Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "1098"} "1,500 metres, Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "1099"} "5,000 metres, Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "1100"} "10,000 metres, Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "1101"} "500 metres, Women"] "\n" [:option {:value "1104"} "1,000 metres, Women"] "\n" [:option {:value "1105"} "1,500 metres, Women"] "\n" [:option {:value "1106"} "3,000 metres, Women"]] [:script {} "$('#same_games_select').change(function(){location.href='/results/' + $('#same_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:form {:class "form-inline form-inline-header"} [:select {:name "id", :id "other_games_select", :class "form-control"} [:option {:value ""} "In other Games"] "\n" [:option {:value "1073"} "1988"] "\n" [:option {:value "1083"} "1992"] "\n" [:option {:value "1093"} "1994"] "\n" [:option {:value "1121"} "2002"] "\n" [:option {:value "1150"} "2006"] "\n" [:option {:value "924527"} "2010"] "\n" [:option {:value "350671"} "2014"] "\n" [:option {:value "9001057"} "2018"] "\n" [:option {:value "19020428"} "2022"] "\n" [:option {:value "15001128"} "2026"]] [:script {} "$('#other_games_select').change(function(){location.href='/results/' + $('#other_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:a {:href "/event_names/541"} "Event type"]] [:h1 {:class "event_title"} "5,000 metres, Women" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "20 February 1998 — 15:00"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/87"} "M-Wave, Nagano"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/1107"} "15 from 9 countries"]]] [:tr {:class "records", :style ""} [:th {:style "vertical-align: top;"} "Olympic Record "] [:td {} [:a {:href "/results/1073"} "7:14.13"] " " [:span {:class "label label-primary"} "WR"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/82921"} "Yvonne van Gennip"] " " [:a {:href "/countries/NED"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/NED.png"}] "NED"] " / 28 February 1988 / " [:a {:href "/records/event_name/541"} "Details"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Starter"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/5002442"} "Heinrich Beck"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GER"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GER.png"}] "GER"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Referee"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/5002698"} "Jan Marmstål"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/SWE"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/SWE.png"}] "SWE"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "As in the 3,000 m, two German women were assumed to duke it out in the 5,000 m. The top favorite was " [:a {:href "/athletes/81514"} "Gunda Niemann-Stirnemann"] ", who already had won the 3,000 m and was runner-up in the 1,500 m. With 7:03.26, Niemann held the World Record, and had won the 1997 World title and 1992 Olympic title. At the only international competition earlier in the season in Innsbruck, Niemann had also won, but she had beaten compatriot " [:a {:href "/athletes/82053"} "Claudia Pechstein"] " by just 0.08. Pechstein had also surprisingly beaten Niemann at this distance at the 1994 Olympics, and was hoping for another upset. Third place was open to a number of contenders, including 1996 and 1997 World Championships runner-up " [:a {:href "/athletes/83120"} "Carla Zijlstra"] " and Italian " [:a {:href "/athletes/80624"} "Elena Belci"] ", having placed 3rd in Innsbruck."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Belci did not prove to be in great shape, and could do no better than 7:15.58. Of the Dutch women, it was not Zijlstra, but " [:a {:href "/athletes/99408"} "Barbara de Loor"] ", who seemed headed for a medal, but she was beaten by the final lap of her opponent in the previous pair, " [:a {:href "/athletes/82130"} "Lyudmila Prokasheva"] ". A strong distance skater from Kazakhstan, Prokasheva had been runner-up at the World Allround Championships in 1995. With her 7:11.14, she would capture the first Olympic speed skating medal for Kazakhstan. Her time was far behind the other two medalists, however."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Gunda Niemann was the first of the Germans to start. On a sound pace - her 3,000 m split would have placed her 4th in that event – she set out to break her own world record. She succeeded, en passant becoming the first woman to complete 5,000 m within 7 minutes: 6:59.65. With this assignment, Pechstein departed in the final pair. Initially slower than Niemann, Pechstein slowly came closer to the leader’s splits. At 3,400 m, she first surpassed Niemann, but her lead remained within a second. Having a 7-tenth lead, she went into the final lap. It appeared to be slower than Niemann’s, but Pechstein held out until the finish line, pushing her skate home in 6:59.61. Pechstein lost her world record a month later to Niemann, who shaved off a second at the World Championships in Calgary."] "\n"] [:button {:id "button735624", :class "btn btn-default"} "Details"] [:script {} "$('#button735624').click(splits)"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Pair"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Time"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "200 m"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "600 m"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "1000 m"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "1400 m"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "1800 m"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "2200 m"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "2600 m"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "3000 m"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "3400 m"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "3800 m"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "4200 m"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "sp